# The Wind in the Willows read whole over nine sittings in the canal town's term. The text is the
# volume wind-in-the-willows; every evidence question is proved against its lines.
lesson reading-wind-in-the-willows v=1 format=book grade=5 unit=1 subject=reading book=wind-in-the-willows levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The Wind in the Willows"
  goal "Read The Wind in the Willows from start to end, and prove what you say about it with lines from the book."

  sitting chapters=[1, 2] {
    show reading.willows-line k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show reading.willows-sum k=0
    level medium hard {
      show reading.willows-word k=0
    }
  }
  sitting chapters=[3, 4] {
    show reading.willows-line k=4 easy-k=3 hard-k=5
    level medium hard {
      show reading.willows-who k=0
    }
    show reading.willows-word k=1
  }
  sitting chapters=[5] {
    show reading.willows-line k=7 easy-k=6 hard-k=8
    show reading.willows-sum k=1
    show reading.willows-why k=0
  }
  sitting chapters=[6, 7] {
    show reading.willows-line k=10 easy-k=9 hard-k=11
    level medium hard {
      show reading.willows-who k=1
    }
    show reading.willows-order k=0
  }
  sitting chapters=[8] {
    show reading.willows-line k=13 easy-k=12 hard-k=14
    level medium hard {
      show reading.willows-sum k=2
    }
    show reading.willows-driver
  }
  sitting chapters=[9] {
    show reading.willows-line k=16 easy-k=15 hard-k=17
    level medium hard {
      show reading.willows-who k=2
    }
    show reading.willows-why k=1
  }
  sitting chapters=[10] {
    show reading.willows-line k=19 easy-k=18 hard-k=20
    level medium hard {
      show reading.willows-sum k=3
    }
    show reading.willows-order k=1
    level hard {
      show reading.willows-knows
    }
  }
  sitting chapters=[11] {
    show reading.willows-line k=22 easy-k=21 hard-k=23
    level medium hard {
      show reading.willows-who k=3
    }
    show reading.willows-word k=2
  }
  sitting chapters=[12] {
    show reading.willows-line k=25 easy-k=24 hard-k=26
    show reading.willows-changed
    show reading.willows-review
  }

  grown-ups """
    The book is read over nine sittings across the canal town's term, one a day, a sitting's chapters
    first and its questions after. Each sitting is a day's reading for a child of ten to twelve at
    about 200 words a minute: chapters 1 and 2 (about 8,500 words, 45 minutes), 3 and 4 (9,100, 45
    minutes), 5 (5,200, 25 minutes), 6 and 7 (8,200, 40 minutes), then one chapter a sitting, 8 to 12
    (4,700 to 6,000 words, 25 to 30 minutes). Reading the first sitting aloud together, or taking
    turns a page each, helps with Grahame's long sentences. Every sitting has a question answered
    with a line number, and the question never uses the line's own words, so a child who skims the
    first page will not find it; the idea to watch for is an answer from memory of the story without
    the line that proves it, so after each answer ask "which words show it?" On paper the family
    reads any edition, and the grown-ups' sheet gives the words of each line beside its number, so
    mark by the words. If a sitting runs long, split it over two days rather than skipping; if a
    chapter is hard going, read its first page aloud and let the child go on alone. Chapter 7, The
    Piper at the Gates of Dawn, is dreamy and slow, and it is fine to read it aloud. The questions
    marked by a grown-up (why the Rat turns back, why the pencil works, whether Toad has changed, and
    the review) have a list of what to notice; talk about them before ticking. Off the page, walk by
    a river or a canal and look for a water vole's hole in the bank, or watch a narrowboat through a
    lock, as Toad's barge travels a canal in chapter 10. The lesson meets China's 2022 standard for
    grades five and six (第三学段, 阅读与鉴赏 6), 「阅读整本书，把握文本的主要内容」, reading a whole book and
    grasping its main content, and Japan's course of study for grades five and six, (3)オ
    「日常的に読書に親しみ」.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level each evidence question asks for a line whose words are close to the question's, most sittings have two questions rather than three, and the who-said and summary questions are left out where a sitting already has a question marked by a grown-up."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level each evidence question asks for what a character feels or does not say, which the line shows without stating, and the chapter 10 sitting adds what the reader knows that the people in the car do not."
  }
}
